# Introduction to DCTAP

```elixir
Mix.install(rudof_elixir: [path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)], kino: "== 0.19.0")
```

## Introduction

This document contains a short introduction to DCTAP using [rudof](https://rudof-project.github.io/).

```elixir
alias Rudof.Session
import ExUnit.Assertions
rudof = Session.new!()
```

## What is DCTAP?

DCTAP (Dublin Core Tabular Application Profiles) is a model that can be used to define metadata using a tabular format.

In this way, it is possible to define models in CSV which can then be converted to other schema technologies like ShEx or SHACL.

## Converting DCTAP to ShEx

Rudof has support for [DCTAP](https://www.dublincore.org/specifications/dctap/) and can be used to read DCTAP files in CSV or Excel files and convert those models to other schema languages.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

DCTAP can be used to represent shapes using a tabular representation using CSV or an spreadsheet format like XLSX. As an example, the following CSV data:

Identifiers use `:Person` rather than bare `Person`: this resolves against rudof's configured empty prefix (`http://example.org/`) instead of the bare-identifier base (`http://default/`). `Session.read_dctap/2` reads CSV source; `Session.read_dctap_file/3` also delegates Excel loading to Rust.

```elixir
profile = ~S"""
shapeId,propertyId,Mandatory,Repeatable,valueDatatype,valueShape
:Person,:name,true,false,xsd:string,
,:birthdate,false,false,xsd:date,
,:worksFor,false,true,,:Company
:Company,:name,true,false,xsd:string,
,:employee,false,true,,:Person
"""

:ok = Session.read_dctap(rudof, profile)
{:ok, json} = Session.serialize_dctap(rudof)
parsed = JSON.decode!(json)
assert length(parsed["shapes"]) == 2
parsed
```

It is possible to convert the DCTAP obtained to ShEx

```elixir
{:ok, schema} = Session.convert_schema(rudof, profile, :dctap, :shex)
IO.puts(schema)
```

## Validating data with the ShEx generated from DCTAP

> Elixir adaptation: The native report should accept Alice and reject Bob’s numeric name. The wrapper returns report maps rather than formatted validation strings.

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_shex(rudof, schema)

:ok =
  Session.read_data(rudof, ~S"""
  PREFIX : <http://example.org/>
  PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
  :alice :name "Alice" ; :birthdate "1970-01-01"^^xsd:date ; :worksFor :acme .
  :acme :name "ACME INC." .
  :bob :name 23 .
  """)

:ok = Session.read_shapemap(rudof, ":alice@:Person, :bob@:Person")
{:ok, report} = Session.validate_shex(rudof)
refute report.conforms
assert Enum.any?(report.entries, &(&1.node =~ "alice" and &1.status == :conformant))
assert Enum.any?(report.entries, &(&1.node =~ "bob" and &1.status == :nonconformant))
Kino.DataTable.new(report.entries)
```

## Visualizing DCTAP content as a UML diagrams

> Elixir adaptation: Rudof's native converter produces PlantUML source. `Rudof.Visualization.plantuml_to_mermaid/1` adapts this generated format for Livebook's Mermaid renderer; the second diagram shows the actual validated RDF data.

```elixir
{:ok, plantuml} =
  Session.show_schema_conversion(rudof, schema, :shex, :uml,
    input_format: :shexc,
    output_format: :uml,
    viz_engine: :plantuml
  )

IO.puts(plantuml)
```

```elixir
{:ok, diagram} = Rudof.Visualization.plantuml_to_mermaid(plantuml)
Kino.Mermaid.new(diagram)
```

```elixir
{:ok, diagram} = Rudof.Visualization.data_to_mermaid(rudof)
Kino.Mermaid.new(diagram)
```

## Close the session

Release the native state when finished. To revisit earlier cells, evaluate from the beginning.

```elixir
:ok = Session.close(rudof)
```
